{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310786,"nonce":"zI7iKPo1XGW/nt5gDDUoVNWIGhc=","title":"Batching the 128k-iter sampler scan into a single Rust extern call was the next real perf win after getFlat. Same template (one FFI call ...","message":"`rayzor_tensor_topk_scan` landed at commit 90252ae. Replaces the\nper-element `tensor.getFlat(i)` + Haxe insertion-sort + repetition-\npenalty loop in `LocalTempSampler.sample` with one FFI call that does\nthe entire 128k-vocab scan in a tight Rust function.\n\n**Why:** post-`getFlat` (8fe809b) profiling showed the sampler scan\nstill dominated decode latency on short prompts. Even at ~5-10 ns per\n`getFlat` call, 128k crossings per token = 0.6-1.3 ms of pure FFI\noverhead. Doing the whole walk + insertion sort + penalty in Rust\ncuts that to one call.\n\n**Measured (M1 Pro, on battery, prior commit was `3914383`):**\n\n**Gate bug caveat:** the first commit 90252ae gated the fast path on\n`owns_data`, which is `false` for every reshape/slice view —\n`lastRow()` always returns one, so the fast path bailed on every sample\ncall and the fallback (= original Haxe scan) ran. The numbers in that\ncommit are noise. **The gate-fix commit `51d1d42` switches to\n`is_contiguous()` and unblocks the real fast path.** Use these numbers:\n\n- long-form Paris-history bench, N=64, RUNS=5:\n  - pre-90252ae baseline:   **14.851 tok/s**, stddev 0.686\n  - 90252ae (strict gate):  15.346 tok/s, stddev 0.365  (fallback)\n  - 51d1d42 (fast path):    **16.677 tok/s**, stddev 0.048\n    → **+12.3% median, 14× tighter stddev** vs baseline.\n- MZ-story 568-token continuation, RUNS=1:\n  - pre-topkScan:   16.397 tok/s\n  - 90252ae:        17.036 tok/s\n  - 51d1d42:        **18.085 tok/s** — +10.3% over baseline.\n- canonical \"What is the capital of France?\" (7-token answer): noisy\n  at this length (7 tokens × battery-throttled decode); skip as a\n  signal, use the N=64 numbers.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- Any Haxe-side loop that does N iterations of `tensor.getFlat(i)` (or\n  similar per-element FFI) over a large contiguous tensor is a candidate\n  for the same batching pattern: define a runtime primitive that takes\n  the tensor + output buffers + scalar params, do the work in Rust,\n  return survivor count or status.\n- The MIR wrapper unpacks Haxe array data pointers via\n  `extract_array_ptr_len` (offset 0 = ptr, offset 8 = len) — same\n  template as `Tensor_zeros` / `Tensor_fromBytesF32`.\n- Output buffers must be pre-allocated by the caller; runtime writes\n  in place. Resizing would force a re-cross into Haxe.\n- Always keep a Haxe fallback for the failure path (non-F32 /\n  non-contiguous) — runtime returns -1, caller routes through the old\n  per-element loop. Preserves correctness for future logits backends.\n\n**FFI hotspots still to consider** (similar batching pattern):\n- KVCache.append: currently routes through `Tensor.appendAlong0` per\n  layer. Already 1 FFI per layer, but the K/V tensors are small\n  (`[seq_q, num_kv_heads, head_dim]` = 1 × 8 × 64 = 512 f32 at decode).\n  Combining 16 layers' K + V appends into a single bulk call could\n  save ~32 FFI calls/token.\n- expandKvHeads: 2× per layer = 32/token. Each one is a stride-aware\n  copy. Could be folded into bmmThreaded so the strided gather happens\n  inside the matmul kernel (one fewer alloc + one fewer FFI per layer).\n- Any future top-p / nucleus sampler should be built on a similar\n  bulk-primitive (`rayzor_tensor_topp_scan`) rather than scanning per\n  element from Haxe.\n\n\nAll numbers above were captured on battery power. macOS aggressively\nthrottles M1 CPU clocks below ~3 GHz when the AC adapter is detached,\nand the throttle isn't uniform across runs (varies with thermal +\nbattery state). For bench A/B comparisons to be meaningful in absolute\nterms, capture both sides under the same power state — ideally on AC\nwith the laptop awake for a few seconds first so the SoC has settled.\n\nRelative deltas (A vs B captured back-to-back under identical\nconditions) survive battery throttling — both sides absorb the same\nslowdown. But the absolute headline numbers (e.g. \"20.5 tok/s on AC vs\n15.3 tok/s on battery\") aren't comparable across power states.\n\n- [[project-optimization-roadmap]] — next pending items now that\n  sampler FFI is addressed\n- [[bugs-llama-chat-streaming-heap-corruption]] — same sampler is the\n  one whose scan got optimised\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_ffi_batching_topk` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}